# Six notes. Three are shown to the default reader, all in the introduction: replica wormholes and islands, Page's
# average entropy, and the RT homology constraint, which the paper's result relaxes. The other three are for students
# and follow the first body paragraph that works with the idea. Listed in reading order.
BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-islands", "after": "section-1-p1", "title": "Replica wormholes and the island formula", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Computed as Hawking did, the entropy of the radiation from an evaporating black hole keeps growing, which for a unitary process cannot go on past the Page time. In 2019 a gravitational replica trick resolved this in simple models. To compute $\operatorname{Tr}\rho_R^n$ for the radiation $R$, one fixes the boundary conditions of $n$ copies and lets gravity fill in the bulk. Besides the solution that fills each copy separately there are replica wormholes, which join the copies through the black hole interior, and at late times they dominate. As $n\to1$ they give the island formula: the entropy is the minimum, over extremal surfaces, of their area over $4G_N$ plus the bulk entropy, where the region counted may include a disconnected island inside the black hole. The result follows Page's curve. The paper finds replica-joining saddles of this kind, with an island covering the horizon, for a static black hole.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-page", "after": "section-1-p2", "title": "Page's average entropy of a subsystem", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Take a Hilbert space $\mathcal H_A\otimes\mathcal H_B$ of dimensions $d_A\le d_B$ and a pure state chosen at random. Page (1993) computed the average entropy of the subsystem $A$: $\overline S\approx\log d_A-d_A/2d_B$. A small subsystem is thus almost maximally entangled, its entropy as large as a thermal one. As $A$ grows past half the system the roles of $A$ and $B$ swap and the entropy falls, because a pure state's two parts always have equal entropy. Plotted against the size of $A$, this rise and symmetric fall is the Page curve. Page used it as a toy model of black hole evaporation, with $A$ the black hole and $B$ the radiation. The paper's question is the same model with an interval of a field theory on a circle as $A$, and the average over its ensemble of pure states plays the role of Page's random choice.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-rt-homology", "after": "section-1-p5", "title": "The RT formula and its homology constraint", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>For a holographic state with a classical bulk dual, the Ryu–Takayanagi formula gives the entropy of a boundary region $A$ as $\text{Area}(X)/4G_N$, minimised over extremal bulk surfaces $X$ anchored on the boundary of $A$. The homology constraint adds that $X$ and $A$ together must bound a region of the bulk slice. In the vacuum it changes nothing: one geodesic serves both $A$ and its complement, and $S(A)=S(A^c)$, as for any pure state. In a black hole background it matters. A surface homologous to $A$ cannot cross the horizon, so the surfaces for $A$ and $A^c$ differ, and for the whole circle the surface is the horizon, giving the Bekenstein–Hawking entropy. That is how the formula knows a thermal state is mixed. Lewkowycz and Maldacena derived the constraint from the replica trick: the surface is the edge of the bulk continuation of the replica cut. The paper's connected saddle changes where that continuation may end.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-euclidean", "after": "sec-ensemble-p2", "title": "Euclidean path integrals for states and traces", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Euclidean time evolution has a path integral kernel: $\langle\phi_1|e^{-\tau H}|\phi_0\rangle$ is the integral over field histories on a strip of Euclidean length $\tau$, with the field fixed to $\phi_0$ and $\phi_1$ at its two edges. The state $e^{-\beta H/2}|\phi_\alpha\rangle$ is then a strip of length $\beta/2$ with $\phi_\alpha$ fixed on one edge and the other edge left open. A density matrix $|\psi\rangle\langle\psi|$ is two such strips. A trace sets the two open edges equal and integrates over their common value: for $\operatorname{Tr}e^{-\beta H}$ this glues a strip of length $\beta$ into a cylinder, and taking the fields smooth across the seam gives the torus of a thermal partition function. Tracing only over $A^c$ glues the strips there and leaves a cut along $A$, the indices of the reduced density matrix. The paper's pictures of states, density matrices and replicas are drawn in this language.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-btz", "after": "sec-termal_GR-p2", "title": "The BTZ black hole and thermal AdS", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A two-dimensional CFT on a circle at inverse temperature $\beta$ lives on a torus. Three-dimensional gravity with negative cosmological constant has two simple solutions with that boundary, both solid tori. In thermal AdS the Euclidean time circle never shrinks, while the spatial circle closes in the bulk. In the Euclidean BTZ black hole the time circle shrinks smoothly to zero at the horizon. For a spatial circle of length $2\pi$ their actions cross at $\beta=2\pi$: thermal AdS dominates at low temperature and BTZ at high temperature, the Hawking–Page transition. In BTZ, a geodesic anchored at the ends of a long boundary interval runs along the horizon, so its length, the entanglement entropy, grows linearly with the interval, like a thermal entropy. The paper works at high temperature, $\beta\ll1$, where BTZ dominates; in thermal AdS it finds no Page transition.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-quenched", "after": "section-4.1-p2", "title": "Averaging logarithms: quenched and annealed", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>When a quantity $Z_\alpha$ varies over an ensemble, the average of its logarithm, $\overline{\log Z}$ (the quenched average), differs in general from the logarithm of its average, $\log\overline Z$ (the annealed one). The two agree when $Z_\alpha$ self-averages, that is when its relative fluctuations are small, as for extensive quantities in large systems. The quenched average can be computed with a second replica trick, $\overline{\log Z}=\lim_{m\to1}\frac{1}{1-m}\log\overline{Z^m}$, which turns it into the average of $m$ coupled copies. If the copies decouple at leading order, $\overline{Z^m}=\overline Z^m$ and the two averages coincide. The paper uses the annealed form for its Rényi entropies; its first appendix argues, with this second replica trick, that the dominant holographic saddle decouples the copies, so the approximation is exact at leading order in $G_N$.</p>"""},
]
